WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.150
Sanitizers are development and CI tools that help expose defects while tests run.

2
00:00:05.150 --> 00:00:13.468
They are especially useful because many memory, lifetime, undefined behavior, leak, and threading issues can stay hidden during normal tests unless

3
00:00:13.468 --> 00:00:15.449
runtime instrumentation makes them visible.

4
00:00:15.449 --> 00:00:22.183
AddressSanitizer focuses on memory access problems such as out-of-bounds access and use-after-lifetime patterns.

5
00:00:22.183 --> 00:00:25.352
UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer highlights operations that violate C++ language rules.

6
00:00:25.352 --> 00:00:27.333
LeakSanitizer helps identify memory leaks.

7
00:00:27.333 --> 00:00:30.502
ThreadSanitizer can reveal data races and synchronization problems.

8
00:00:30.502 --> 00:00:32.483
Each serves a different purpose.

9
00:00:32.483 --> 00:00:41.197
Sanitizer findings should be treated as real engineering defects unless a team has a documented, reviewed reason to classify a specific finding

10
00:00:41.197 --> 00:00:41.594
differently.

11
00:00:41.594 --> 00:00:45.951
Ignoring sanitizer output teaches the team to distrust its own evidence.

12
00:00:45.951 --> 00:00:48.724
Triage should be prompt, owned, and visible.

13
00:00:48.724 --> 00:00:51.497
Dedicated CI jobs make sanitizer coverage repeatable.

14
00:00:51.497 --> 00:00:59.815
Some teams run sanitizer builds on every pull request for selected targets, while others use scheduled jobs or focused test suites.

15
00:00:59.815 --> 00:01:09.719
The right schedule depends on build time and project risk, but the work should be part of the workflow rather than a rare manual event.

16
00:01:09.719 --> 00:01:13.284
Sanitizer builds are not usually production hardening by default.

17
00:01:13.284 --> 00:01:17.245
They can change performance, memory layout, runtime dependencies, and behavior.

18
00:01:17.245 --> 00:01:21.206
Their primary role is defect discovery during development and testing.

19
00:01:21.206 --> 00:01:25.168
Release hardening is a separate build profile with different goals.

20
00:01:25.168 --> 00:01:29.129
Sanitizer value depends on meaningful tests and realistic execution paths.

21
00:01:29.129 --> 00:01:31.506
Instrumentation only observes code that runs.

22
00:01:31.506 --> 00:01:38.240
Boundary tests, parser tests, integration tests, concurrency tests, and representative workloads make sanitizer results far more useful.
